WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.797
After identifying a duplicate group, we may need to choose a preferred record, sometimes called the survivor.

00:00:06.797 --> 00:00:10.042
That choice should follow an explicit rule.

00:00:10.042 --> 00:00:16.007
One option is completeness: prefer the record with more useful populated fields.

00:00:16.007 --> 00:00:21.567
Another is recency: prefer a newer trusted record when that makes sense.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:27.405
A third is source priority: if one system is considered authoritative, prefer its record.

00:00:28.148 --> 00:00:35.478
And finally, use a stable tie-breaker, such as the record identifier, so the result is reproducible.

00:00:35.478 --> 00:00:39.588
Imagine two duplicate records with the same name and email.

00:00:39.588 --> 00:00:43.323
One has a phone and address, while the other does not.

00:00:43.323 --> 00:00:47.058
A completeness rule may prefer the first record.

00:00:47.058 --> 00:00:50.668
The key is that the decision should be explainable.

00:00:50.668 --> 00:00:56.024
We should never choose a survivor simply because one row happened to appear first.
